  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/d2/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_100.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "[RECAP: The sinister Mr. Hyde has committed crimes while impersonating Thor, in order to turn the general populace against him!]\n\nThe city hurls insults at Thor for turning against its citizens, and the police even start shooting at him.&nbsp; He puts two and two together and gets \"Hyde.\"&nbsp; He has to get out of the public eye, so he reverts to Don Blake, only to be reminded by Jane that he promised to take her out to dinner for her birthday, at the Ritz Terrace, but Mr. Hyde has overheard the time and location and crashes their date, holding them both at gunpoint and forcing them into Don's car!\n\nHe orders them to go to his abandoned old castle.&nbsp; Don is sure that he won't be letting them leave now that he's revealed where he lives!&nbsp; Once there, Hyde ties up Blake and sets a 24-hour time bomb next to him so that if anything happens to him during that time, Don Blake is doomed!&nbsp; Then he takes Jane and leaves, heading for the East River Naval Yard, where he plans to steal a Polaris submarine, after which he intends to \"roam the Seven Seas like a <i>king</i>... and <i>you</i> shall become my queen!\"\n\nMeanwhile, Don is gradually able to stretch out a hand to touch his cane and strike it, becoming Thor, immediately soaring out to try and find Mr. Hyde and Jane.&nbsp; Thor sees the commotion at the East River Naval Yard and lands on the Polaris sub that Hyde is about to board.&nbsp; Meanwhile, in Asgard, Odin decides to check in on Thor and watches his confrontation with Mr. Hyde.\n\nThor and Hyde briefly tussle before Hyde descends into the submarine, taking Jane with him.&nbsp; Thor easily tears through the hatch and engages Hyde below, but Jane, fearful that Blake will die if something happens to Hyde, covers his dropped hammer with a piece of canvas, delaying Thor from finding it!\n\nEven without his hammer, Thor creates a tornado with his cape, masking his transformation back to Blake, who then searches the area and eventually finds the concealed hammer, all under the cover of the brief whirlwind.&nbsp; Thor rises and prepares to strike Hyde, but Jane stops him, pleading to let Hyde escape so that nothing happens to Don Blake.&nbsp; Thor has to let him jump to safety into the depths and promise Jane that he'll free Blake.&nbsp; (Jane wonders how he knows where to look.)\n\nThe police show up, and \"suddenly\" they realize that Hyde was impersonating him.&nbsp; However, more importantly, Odin was watching Jane's attempt to thwart Thor's battle against Mr. Hyde, and he's not happy!&nbsp; \"You <i>dared</i> ask me to make <i>that</i> female immortal? ... Petition denied!&nbsp; She is not worthy!\"&nbsp; Thor has no time to explain before Odin disappears, making Thor's victory seem more hollow than ever.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Castles, time bombs, and pirate fantasies make for a very uneven adventure.\nBut Jane Foster unknowingly sabotaging her own future keeps the issue afloat.",
  "subtitle": "How Not to Impress Odin",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | NotACrook | width=6%] The action picks up about where you'd expect, and I thought the creators did a good job with the recap (being necessary due to the fact that this was the conclusion to one of Marvel's very first \"continued\" stories).&nbsp; Thor realizes he's been impersonated and figures Mr. Hyde is responsible.\n\n[ASSET: left | HeavenBeingWithYou | width=13%] Seeing Don and Jane at dinner together, even so briefly, [SPAN: severity=success; text=was a positive.]&nbsp; They're clearly crazy about each other and even <i>hint</i> at this in their conversation (e.g. \"it's always heaven being with you!\").\n[BR]\n[BR] But it also struck me as <i>odd</i> that only three issues ago (in [LINK:1963:JIM_97_thor0|JIM #97|2026-12-16]), Jane left Dr. Blake to go work for someone else, only to realize that Blake had more courage than she gave him credit for (and return in the very next issue).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Their dinner conversation doesn't even <i>mention</i> the incident.]\n\nBut the Mr. Hyde intrusion on their dinner date only goes to show that Thor is not the only thing on the villain's mind.&nbsp; He had originally gone after Don Blake (the motivation of which I questioned in my last review), so at least he continues with that vendetta here, essentially kidnapping them while they're at the fancy restaurant.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nHyde takes them to his home, which is... <i>where else?</i>... an \"old deserted castle.\"&nbsp; One wonders many things when stories feature castles, such as:\n\n[ASSET: right | AnotherCastle | width=21%] [HANG] * Where do all these old castles keep coming from?\n[HANG] * Why is the moon <b>always full</b> when castles are depicted in Marvel Comics?\n[HANG] * How is there one at the \"top of that hill\" near New York City?\n[HANG] * If NYC ever <i>did</i> have nearby castles, how did they <i>remain</i> abandoned for long?[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Are they <i>haunted</i> or something?)]\n[HANG] * How did Hyde's home transform into a castle after we were shown his <i>mansion</i> on page 11 of the previous issue?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Bet you <i>forgot</i> about that one!)]\n[HANG] * And one unrelated question:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Where does one even <i>get</i> a time bomb?]\n\nBut the absurdities continue...\n\nHyde's master plan to \"roam the Seven Seas like a <i>king</i>\" and even force Jane Foster to become his queen is the very epitome of... [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=random.]&nbsp; Where did <i>this</i> urge come from?\n\nMy guess is that the last issue never left readers with the impression that his newfound strength would lead him to become a [SPAN: severity=error; text=pirate.]&nbsp; On the contrary, once he obtained his brute strength, he said (on last issue's page 5) \"...with my new-found strength, what massive crimes I could commit!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Now... <i>suddenly</i>... you're content to roam the Seven Seas?]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut despite the story's weak moments, its <b>key moment</b> is found on page 11, where Stan and his artist show Jane Foster, fearful for Don Blake's safety because of the time bomb, covering Thor's hammer in an effort to delay Thor and allow time for Mr. Hyde to escape.\n[ASSET: center | KeyMoment | width=67%]\n\nJane can <i>hardly</i> be blamed for not knowing that her action threatened to jeopardize the very man she claimed to love (and can be forgiven for <i>that much</i> at least), and if the action reveals anything, it's that [SPAN: severity=info; text=her feelings for Don Blake are stronger than any she may have harbored for the Thunder God in the past] [SPAN: severity=success; text=(which is good to know)].\n\n[ASSET: left | LetHydeEscape | width=22%] But at the same time, her action (and subsequent pleas to Thor to allow Hyde to escape) dooms her chances of becoming an immortal in the eyes of Odin (who has been monitoring the battle from up in Asgard).\n[BR]\n[BR] In Odin's defense, he saw only a mortal woman [SPAN: severity=error; text=actively sabotaging] his son's battle with Hyde long enough to allow the villain to escape.&nbsp; In his mind, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=this is unpardonable!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIn the end:\n[HANG] * Mr. Hyde escapes into the waters of the East River\n[HANG] * Thor heads off to \"rescue\" Don Blake [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(good thing, because I was going to ask whatever happened to that <i>bomb</i>)]\n[HANG] * Jane is left to wonder how Thor knows where Blake even is\n[HANG] * Thor's victory is hollow\n\n[ASSET: right | AmazingIntuition | width=13%] The police somehow conclude that Hyde was impersonating Thor, and the book never explains how (and my own eyeballs widened at the dismissive way Stan wrote that <i>completely</i> out of the story, especially after it dominated the first two pages).\n[BR]\n[BR] What on Earth led them to this intuitive conclusion?&nbsp; It <i>couldn't</i> just have been the sight of Mr. Hyde diving into the waters of the East River after escaping his battle with Thor... could it?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=The casual wave-of-the-hand dismissal was a negative the book seemed determined to ignore.]\n\n[ASSET: left | HeckArtwork | width=7%] As for the artwork, I thought Don Heck did a solid job, and even if the credit's <i>hadn't</i> listed him as artist, panels like this one (the middle one on page 4) would have given it away!\n[BR]\n[BR] Heck's thin lines and sharper angles (and <i>very</i> distinctive mouths) stand out here more than some of his recent work.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nWhile I would hardly call the story worthy of a magazine's 100<sup>th</sup> issue (and I <i>do</i> realize it's not <b>Thor's</b> 100<sup>th</sup> issue), I still walked away from the book mostly satisfied, mainly because of the title's attention to the whole [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Can Jane Foster become an immortal?\"] plot line.\n\nThe <i>mechanics</i> of the story [SPAN: severity=error; text=left much to be desired], such as why Hyde returned his attention to Don Blake immediately after the previous issue appeared to shift toward his campaign against Thor.&nbsp; There was also the recurrence of the old, abandoned castle trope (which is getting tiresome -- where on <i>Earth</i> do these castles keep coming from?), not to mention the astonishing, out-of-left-field insight of the police that Thor was innocent on the book's final page.\n\nBut say what you will about those [SPAN: severity=error; text=weaker moments,] seeing Don and Jane share a happy moment together (for once) was [SPAN: severity=success; text=nice.]&nbsp; And Odin's outrage over seeing Jane trying to prevent Thor from hurting Hyde was [SPAN: severity=success; text=completely understandable.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=These two important plot beats ultimately allowed the book to grade higher than it normally would have.]\n\nI actually thought this issue was better than the last, even though it will never cement itself as particularly memorable.&nbsp; This was perhaps more noticeable because of all the hype at the end of last month about this being the big 100<sup>th</sup> issue.&nbsp; It <i>was</i>, but the story didn't seem more special than what you'd expect from any <i>other</i> issue.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=But it at least moves the ongoing saga forward, which invites readers to return next month, even <i>without</i> an actual cliffhanger ending.]",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:</b>  \"The Storm Giants\" (a story about the boyhood of Thor)\n\nBoy Thor and brother Loki sneak into the castle of the \"thundering Storm Giants.\"&nbsp; They've stolen some of the apples of Iduna.&nbsp; Both boys are tiny in comparison to the giants.&nbsp; Loki pushes Thor into full view of the giants, and a scuffle ensues, with Thor and Loki finally escaping from them.&nbsp; Every time Thor succeeds, he is able to lift Odin's hammer just a little more.&nbsp; Loki was hoping he would get credit.\n\nIt's a sub-par plot, but gives us a little taste of the two as boys.&nbsp; Nothing really notable here."
  ],
  "ownership": "I was lucky enough to get a FN/VF copy of this as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $18, finally selling it on eBay in May 2020 for $97.50, netting me almost $68.\n\n[DEVNOTE: Another tape-covered copy in much worse condition, I've elected to keep.]",
  "highlight": "Thor defeats neither Mr. Hyde nor fate when Jane's intervention draws Odin's wrath."
}